#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sync-skills — validate the in-repo `skills/` copy of the chopshop-studio
skill and document the HOST-SPECIFIC tagging convention.

NOTE (2026-09-28): the `chopshop-studio` skill was once GENERATED from an
on-disk Hermes working skill of the same name. That disk skill has been
retired (its content now lives in the Hermes skills
`chopshop-studio-ui-verification` and `chopshop-studio-runner-kind`, plus this
repo's own `docs/`). The in-repo `skills/chopshop-studio/` is now the source of
truth and is maintained IN-REPO — so there is nothing left to generate, and
this script's `GENERATED` list is empty on purpose.

The HOST-SPECIFIC tagging convention below is still in force for the in-repo
skill: everything that names this box's paths/ports/hostnames carries the
`HOST-SPECIFIC` token so a reader can tell deployment facts apart from
host-agnostic knowledge, and knows what to remove or generalise before reuse
elsewhere. The banner maps are retained as the machine-readable record of which
sections/references are host-specific.

USAGE
    ./sync-skills            validate the in-repo skill (no-op while GENERATED is empty)
    ./sync-skills --check    report drift only (change nothing)
    ./sync-skills --diff     show what would change

BEFORE PUBLISHING, RUN --diff: A PUBLISH OVERWRITES REPO-SIDE EDITS.

    The one-way rule below says the on-disk skill is the source of truth and a
    repo copy is never imported back. That holds for the LITERALS, but it is not
    a licence to publish blind, because the repo copy has been hand-edited
    directly more than once -- `skills/chopshop-studio/references/candidate-triage.md`
    was updated in-repo across three commits (`9987ac3`, `b68e55e`, `ec80477`,
    each touching that one file, which is what distinguishes a hand edit from a
    publish) to record P1-P5 as shipped, while the on-disk skill still said
    "Next phases (P1-P5) are unstarted". A publish would have reverted every word
    of that.

    So the workflow is: `--diff`, read it, fold anything the repo copy knows and
    the disk copy does not back INTO the on-disk skill, THEN publish. The
    redaction cannot cause that loss -- it only rewrites host literals -- but
    regeneration from a stale source can, and a unified diff is the only cheap
    way to see it.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import difflib
import os
import re
import shutil
import sys

REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
SKILLS_DIR = os.path.join(REPO, "skills")
HERMES_SKILLS = os.path.expanduser("~/.hermes/skills")

